p.src:
An equal-weight state provides the first useful correction at the board. The column $\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}$ has squared norm $2$, not $1$, and therefore is not normalized. Dividing by $\sqrt2$ repairs it: $\ket{\rightarrow}
  =
  \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
  \begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}
  =
  \frac{\ket{+}+\ket{-}}{\sqrt2}.$ It gives $P(+)=P(-)=1/2$ in the vertical detector and corresponds to one horizontal preparation. The correction from $(1,1)^T$ to $2^{-1/2}(1,1)^T$ is not cosmetic: normalization is what turns squared amplitudes into a complete probability distribution.

